    "reading": {
        "body": "Massachusetts has 191 tracked bills, with a dense cluster on AI and data regulation, including competing privacy acts, algorithmic rent bans, synthetic media disclosure, and a study of AI-driven load growth.\n\nThe pattern is duplication rather than consolidation. Multiple bills propose versions of a Massachusetts data privacy act, and algorithmic rent setting, facial recognition limits, election misinformation, and AI in healthcare each appear in several House and Senate filings. This suggests broad legislative interest but no single agreed framework yet.\n\nThe data also connects AI to physical infrastructure. One bill would establish a commission to study load growth due to AI and data centers, another targets emissions from artificial intelligence, and several address energy affordability and clean power. These sit alongside economic development and workforce training measures, so the state is weighing both the costs and the competitive benefits of AI.\n\nThese filings do not show outcome. They record bill titles and filing dates, not committee action, votes, or enactment. Many entries are procedural, such as study orders or site information links, so the count overstates the number of substantive proposals that will reach law.",
